  "documentation": {
    "range": "The Goblin Shark has a patchy yet widespread global distribution across the Atlantic and Indo-Pacific Oceans (Ebert <em>et al</em>. 2013). The Northwest Pacific (Japan and Taiwan) appears to be a hotspot of occurrence (Duffy&#160;<em>et al.</em>&#160;2004, Yano&#160;<em>et al.&#160;</em>2007). The species is likely to occur in more locations than presently known as surveys are undertaken in other regions or as deepwater fisheries expand globally.",
    "population": "Population size and trends for this species are unknown. Catch records from Japan (where many of the known individuals have been reported from) dating back to its discovery in 1898 show no trends in abundance (Yano<em> et al. </em>2007). It is possible to infer from the limited fisheries interactions across its range that this species largely occupies areas outside fisheries, although it may also be naturally rare.",
    "habitats": "The Goblin Shark is recorded on outer continental shelves, upper slopes and off seamounts at depths of 0-1,300 m, but has been reported mainly at depths between 270-960 m&#160;(Ebert<span class=\"apple-converted-space\">&#160;<em>et al.</em><span class=\"apple-converted-space\">&#160;2013). It also appears to be mesopelagic. Maximum measured size is 410 cm total length (TL) (Kukuev 1982), however, estimates from photographs of&#160;a specimen taken in the Gulf of Mexico&#160;suggest&#160;540-617 cm TL (Parsons<em>&#160;et al</em>. 2002). Males mature between 260-380 cm TL; female maturity &gt;400 cm TL; size at birth 80-90 cm TL (Ebert <em>et al</em>. 2013). Pregnant females are unknown but like other lamniform sharks, the embryos are probably oviphagous and litter size is likely to be small. Nothing else is known about its biology.&#160;<span data-dobid=\"hdw\"></span>",
    "threats": "<p>The Goblin Shark is rarely caught as incidental bycatch in deepwater benthic gillnet, benthic longline, trawl, and surface drift net fisheries, and may also be entangled in deepwater fishing gear. Most reported captures are juveniles suggesting that the bulk of the adult population occurs outside the depth range of, or is otherwise unavailable to most deepwater fisheries (Duffy <em>et al.</em> 2004).&#160;</p><p>Most captures have been reported from Japan (Tokyo Submarine Canyon), where subadults (&lt;200 cm total length) have been taken in benthic gillnets between 100-350 m depth (Yano <em>et al. </em>2007).&#160;In an unusual occurrence, an exceptionally large number (&gt;100) were reportedly caught off the northwest coast of Taiwan at depths ~600 m in April 2003 following a strong earthquake in the area&#160;(Duffy&#160;<em>et al.</em>&#160;2004).&#160;</p>",
    "measures": "Further information is required on the biology, population size and trends, and interactions with fisheries across the range of the Goblin Shark. There are currently no species-specific management actions in place for the species.",
    "use_trade": "The flesh of the Goblin Shark is generally not marketed, but its jaws are highly sought after by collectors (Ebert&#160;<em>et al</em>. 2013).",
    "rationale": "<p>The Goblin Shark (<em>Mitsukurina owstoni</em>) is a large deepwater shark (to 410 cm total length (TL), possibly 617 cm TL), with a widespread but patchy global distribution across the Atlantic and Indo-Pacific Oceans. It has been recorded on outer continental shelves, upper slopes and off seamounts at depths of 0-1,300 m, but mainly at depths between 270-960 m. It also appears to be mesopelagic. Population size and trends for this species are unknown. The Goblin Shark is rarely caught as incidental bycatch in deepwater benthic gillnet, benthic longline, trawl, and surface drift net fisheries, with most captures reported from Japan. There are no species-specific management actions in place at this time. Although there is a lack of information on its biology and population size and trends, it is possible to infer from the limited fisheries interactions across its range that this species occupies areas outside those available to fisheries, although it may also be naturally rare. At this time, there is nothing to infer any population decline and the Goblin Shark is assessed as Least Concern.&#160;</p>",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": "There is a single extant species in the family Mitsukurinidae."
  },
